--TEST--
A `///` run attaches to the declaration below it and changes nothing about the program that carries it, while `//` and `////` document nothing — `rule:tooling/doc-comment-is-three-slashes`, `rule:tooling/doc-comment-attaches-to-the-next-declaration`, `rule:tooling/doc-comment-tags-are-see-and-example`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:programs/doc-comments
/// A greeting, and the text a reader of it sees.
///
/// `rule:tooling/doc-comment-attaches-to-the-next-declaration`: the lines
/// above and this one are a single comment however many there are, and the
/// declaration under them is what they document.
///
/// @see Greeting::render
/// @example examples/greeting.nvs
class Greeting {
    /// The text this greeting carries.
    public string $text;

    /// Builds a greeting over its own text.
    public function constructor(string $text) {
        $this->text = $text;
    }

    /// The greeting as a reader sees it.
    ///
    /// There is no `@param` and no `@return` here on purpose: the signature is
    /// on the line below, and a signature cannot drift from itself.
    ///
    /// @see Greeting::$text
    public function render(): string {
        return $this->text;
    }

    // An ordinary comment documents nothing, and the walk that resolves a doc
    // comment's tags never sees one. A private member is outside
    // `rule:tooling/strict-docs` as well, so nothing here is owed a `///`.
    private function hidden(): string {
        return "";
    }
}

//// Four slashes are an ordinary comment too, as in Rust. `///` is the marker,
//// and a longer run of them is a divider somebody drew.

var $greeting = new Greeting("a documented declaration runs unchanged");
echo $greeting->render(), "\n";
echo "two slashes are not documentation\n";
echo "four slashes are not documentation\n";
--FILE examples/greeting.nvs--
<?nvs
// The file the `@example` above names. A tag's target has to exist and has to
// sit in a directory the test corpus walks, so the case writes one beside the
// program that documents itself with it.
echo "a greeting\n";
--EXPECT--
a documented declaration runs unchanged
two slashes are not documentation
four slashes are not documentation
